---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { tools } from '../data/tools';
import '../styles/article.css';

// The tool list below is registry-driven (§3.3), so tool six appears here
// without a copy edit. The prose around it must not name a count.

const TITLE = 'About | ReflexLab';
const DESCRIPTION =
  'Learn about ReflexLab: free reaction time, click speed, and memory tests that track your best, ranks, and trend. No accounts, results stay in your browser.';
// The live page's og:description deliberately differs from its meta
// description — preserved as-is (§7).
const OG_DESCRIPTION =
  'Learn about ReflexLab - free reflex and performance tests built to help you measure, practice, and improve your speed over time.';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={TITLE}
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  ogDescription={OG_DESCRIPTION}
  path="/about"
  topbarLabel="About"
>
  <article class="article-container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span class="breadcrumbs-separator">/</span>
        <span class="breadcrumbs-current">About</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="article-title">About ReflexLab</h1>
      <p class="article-subtitle">
        Reflex and performance tests that keep score, so you can see whether you are improving.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-content">
      <p class="article-intro">
        ReflexLab began as one reaction time test, built for gaming and sport, where a tenth of a
        second decides the duel. It has grown into a set of tests, and each one keeps a record of
        every attempt so you can tell a real gain from a good day.
      </p>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>What You Can Measure</h2>
        <p>Every test gives you one number to chase and stores the settings you set it under.</p>
        <ul class="article-list">
          {
            tools.map((tool) => (
              <li>
                <a href={tool.href}>{tool.name}</a>: {tool.blurb}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p>
          The click tests share one rank table on purpose. Eight clicks per second means the same
          thing whether you hit it on the timed <a href="/cps">CPS test</a>, the ten-second{' '}
          <a href="/kohi-click-test">Kohi format</a>, or with your{' '}
          <a href="/right-click-cps-test">right mouse button</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>What Happens After a Run</h2>
        <p>
          Finish a run and the panel under the test shows your personal best, your session average,
          a trend graph, and a timestamped history of recent attempts. Ranks climb from Beginner to
          Elite. Clear a tier once and it stays ticked on your checklist, even if the next ten runs
          fall short of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <a href="/memory-test">visual memory test</a> uses milestones instead of ranks. A level
          number does not band into tiers that mean anything, so it marks checkpoints you either reached
          or have not reached yet.
        </p>
        <p>
          Hitting Reset clears a test's best, history, and ranks. Your settings survive it, so you
          do not have to rebuild them to start over.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Honest Measurement</h2>
        <p>
          A reaction test is worth no more than its clock. ReflexLab stamps the time with
          <code>performance.now()</code> at the instant the target flips, stamps it again inside your
          input handler, and does nothing in between that could add delay.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your monitor, your mouse, and your browser still add a few milliseconds on top. Compare
          your score against your own history rather than against a laboratory. The reaction test
          also runs five rounds by default and scores the average, so a single lucky click never
          sets your personal best.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Private by Default</h2>
        <p>
          No account, no email, no sign-in. Your results sit in your browser's localStorage and go
          nowhere else. Your scores will not follow you to another browser or another device, and
          clearing site data erases them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> covers what gets stored and what analytics see.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Guides</h2>
        <p>
          Knowing what moves your reaction time helps you train instead of guess. The{' '}
          <a href="/guides">guides</a> cover how sleep and caffeine change your numbers, and which practice
          methods hold up.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-cta">
        <h2>See Where You Stand</h2>
        <p>
          Start with the reaction time test. Five rounds gives you an average, a rank, and a number
          to beat next time.
        </p>
        <a href="/" class="btn btn-primary">Take the Test</a>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="disclaimer-section">
    <p class="disclaimer">
      <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> ReflexLab's tests are built for entertainment and personal tracking.
      Your device, browser, input hardware, and display refresh rate all shape the numbers you see. For
      clinical or research measurement, use calibrated laboratory equipment.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .disclaimer-section {
    padding: var(--space-xl) 0 0;
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--line-subtle);
  }

  .disclaimer {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    font-size: var(--text-label-size);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .article-content code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--ink-primary);
    background: var(--panel-02);
    border: 1px solid var(--line-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
  }
</style>
